\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Binary tetrahedral spinor gauge fields}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Let #{G=2T\subset SU(2)} be the
[24 Hurwitz units](fgap-000K). Use its defining complex
spinor representation #{\sigma}, with
#{\chi_\sigma(h)=2\operatorname{Re}h}, for both the Wilson action
and the normalized Wilson loops of \ref{fdip-006V}.
The [complete character table](fdip-0053) has irreducibles
#{1,\lambda,\lambda^2,\sigma,\sigma\lambda,
\sigma\lambda^2,\tau}, with degrees #{1,1,1,2,2,2,3}.
Their squared degrees sum to #{24}. The nonreal characters are
kept separately as complex irreducibles here; no real isotypic
multiplicity is substituted into the surface formula.}

\p{Put #{r=e^{-\beta /2}} and
#{\omega=(-1+i+j+k)/2}. In the class order of that table,
##{\begin{array}{c|rrrrrrr}
h&1&-1&i&\omega&-\omega&\omega^2&-\omega^2\\\hline
|[h]|&1&1&6&4&4&4&4\\
f_\beta(h)&1&r^4&r^2&r^3&r&r^3&r
\end{array}}
The resulting character coefficients are
##{\begin{aligned}
a&=\frac{1+r^4+6r^2+8r+8r^3}{24} &&(1),\\
b&=\frac{(1-r)^4}{24} &&(\lambda,\lambda^2),\\
c&=\frac{(1-r^2)(1+r^2+4r)}{12} &&(\sigma),\\
d&=\frac{(1-r^2)(1-r)^2}{12}
 &&(\sigma\lambda,\sigma\lambda^2),\\
e&=\frac{(1-r^2)^2}{8} &&(\tau).
\end{aligned}}
To verify them, let #{s_j=\zeta^j+\zeta^{2j}}, where
#{\zeta=e^{2\pi i/3}}. Thus #{s_0=2} and #{s_1=s_2=-1}.
The class-weighted inner products give
##{\begin{aligned}
c_{\lambda^j}&=\frac{1+r^4+6r^2+4s_j(r+r^3)}{24},\\
c_{\sigma\lambda^j}&=\frac{1-r^4+2s_j(r-r^3)}{12},\\
c_\tau&=\frac{3+3r^4-6r^2}{24}.
\end{aligned}}
Factoring these expressions gives the displayed coefficients. They
are nonnegative for #{\beta\ge0}, and
#{a+2b+2c+4d+3e=1}. At #{\beta=0}, only #{a=1} remains;
as #{\beta\to\infty}, each coefficient tends to its degree
divided by #{24}, the character expansion of the identity indicator.}

\p{For the [closed oriented surface](fdip-006W),
##{\begin{aligned}
Z_\Sigma(f_\beta)={}&a^P+2b^P\\
 &+2^{2-2g}\left[\left(\frac c2\right)^P
       +2\left(\frac d2\right)^P\right]\\
 &+3^{2-2g}\left(\frac e3\right)^P.
\end{aligned}}
Unnormalized counting multiplies this by #{24^{|E|}}.
These powers use complex irreducible dimensions; the quaternionic
nature of the spinor does not change its complex degree two.}

\p{On the [one-face torus](fdip-006X), set #{g=1}, #{P=1}.
Substitution gives
##{Z_{\TT^2}(f_\beta)
 =a+2b+\frac c2+d+\frac e3
 =\frac{7+16r^2+r^4}{24}.}
For its face loop, #{W_\sigma=\operatorname{Re}H} and
#{f_\beta(H)=e^{-\beta(1-W_\sigma)}}. Differentiating the finite
sum therefore gives
##{\langle W_\sigma\rangle
 =1+\frac{d}{d\beta}\log Z_{\TT^2}(f_\beta)
 =\frac{7-r^4}{7+16r^2+r^4}.}
This differs from the quaternion-group answer, despite using the
same spinor trace convention. The calculation concerns the specified
finite gauge group and plaquette; it does not identify the finite
model with an #{SU(2)} continuum theory.}
